WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.246
Hi, I'm Umbel!

00:00:02.246 --> 00:00:06.388
Ant Ling released five speech models, and one small engine does the talking.

00:00:06.388 --> 00:00:07.981
Here's how it writes sound.

00:00:09.808 --> 00:00:13.930
A language model writes continuous audio frames, twelve and a half a second.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:17.102
And every voice control is just text in its prompt.

00:00:18.717 --> 00:00:23.202
The words and the controls go into a small language model as ordinary text.

00:00:23.622 --> 00:00:27.726
A small diffusion transformer turns its hidden state into one audio frame,

00:00:27.726 --> 00:00:29.436
in ten steps from noise.

00:00:29.856 --> 00:00:32.915
That frame is sixty-four numbers, eighty milliseconds of sound.

00:00:32.915 --> 00:00:35.634
It goes back in, and the loop repeats.

00:00:36.054 --> 00:00:39.342
When a stop head says done, a decoder turns all the frames

00:00:39.342 --> 00:00:40.986
into a waveform in one pass.

00:00:43.025 --> 00:00:46.893
The usual way turns audio into discrete codes and has the model

00:00:46.893 --> 00:00:48.182
pick from a vocabulary.

00:00:48.602 --> 00:00:50.082
Ming skips the vocabulary.

00:00:50.082 --> 00:00:53.042
A flow head draws each continuous frame directly.

00:00:55.008 --> 00:00:58.944
On the left, text tokens, controls and an optional speaker embedding.

00:00:59.364 --> 00:01:02.928
Above the model, the DiT patch head and the decoder, chunk by chunk.

00:01:04.358 --> 00:01:06.716
The audio models are built around that engine.

00:01:07.136 --> 00:01:09.120
A Whisper-shaped encoder hears the input.

00:01:09.623 --> 00:01:12.429
A sparse mixture of experts reads it and writes text.

00:01:12.849 --> 00:01:15.691
And the talker that speaks the answer is the same small

00:01:15.691 --> 00:01:16.724
model, byte for byte.

00:01:18.467 --> 00:01:19.864
The name counts the backbone.

00:01:19.864 --> 00:01:22.657
The whole checkpoint holds about one and a half billion.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:29.292
On a busy four-core machine it ran five to ten times slower

00:01:29.292 --> 00:01:32.099
than real time, and Whisper heard every English word.

00:01:34.308 --> 00:01:38.120
One small loop of continuous frames, with controls written as text, is

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.026
the voice of the whole release.

00:01:42.233 --> 00:01:43.395
Frames, not codes.

00:01:43.395 --> 00:01:44.558
Controls as text.

00:01:44.558 --> 00:01:46.883
One engine, speaking for three models.

00:01:47.303 --> 00:01:49.344
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:49.344 --> 00:01:49.927
I'm Umbel.

00:01:49.927 --> 00:01:50.218
Bye!
